1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,150
Chapter 51: Obesity.

3
00:00:05,150 --> 00:00:17,587
General Considerations Pregnancy And Obesity Antepartum Management Intrapartum Management Bariatric Surgery References Extensive weight gain is a major health problem today in many affluent societies.

4
00:00:17,587 --> 00:00:30,075
The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) (2020) reported the prevalence in the United States during 2017 to 2018 to be 42 percent among all adults.

5
00:00:30,075 --> 00:00:40,337
The adverse health aspects of obesity are staggering and include increased risks for type 2 diabetes, heart disease, hypertension, and osteoarthritis.

6
00:00:40,337 --> 00:00:49,637
Importantly, obese women and their fetuses are predisposed to numerous pregnancy-related complications and to longterm morbidity and mortality.

7
00:00:49,637 --> 00:00:52,000
General Considerations.

8
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:55,012
# Definitions and Prevalence.

9
00:00:55,012 --> 00:01:03,812
Of systems to classify obesity, the body mass index (BMI), also known as the Quetelet index, is most often used.

10
00:01:03,812 --> 00:01:11,437
The BMI is calculated as weight in kilograms divided by the square of the height in meters (kg/m2).

11
00:01:11,437 --> 00:01:16,837
Calculated BMI values are available in various chart and graphic forms .

12
00:01:16,837 --> 00:01:37,387
The National Institutes of Health (2000) classifies adults according to BMI as follows: normal is 18.5 to 24.9 kg/m2, overweight is 25 to 29.9 kg/m2, and obese is ≥30 kg/m2.

13
00:01:37,387 --> 00:01:53,600
Obesity is further divided into: class 1 is 30 to 34.9 kg/m2, class 2 is 35 to 39.9 kg/m2, and class 3 is ≥40 kg/m2.

14
00:01:53,600 --> 00:02:04,112
Class 3 obesity is often referred to as morbid obesity, and supermorbid obesity describes a BMI ≥50 kg/m2.

15
00:02:04,112 --> 00:02:06,700
Figure 51-1.

16
00:02:06,700 --> 00:02:10,775
Chart for estimating body mass index (BMI).

17
00:02:10,775 --> 00:02:17,925
To find the BMI category for a particular subject, locate the point at which the height and weight intersect.

18
00:02:17,925 --> 00:02:29,562
Using these definitions, from 2015 to 2016 in the United States, among girls and women, the prevalence of obesity rose with age and varied among ethnicities .

19
00:02:29,562 --> 00:02:34,075
Overall, severity also advances with increasing poverty.

20
00:02:34,075 --> 00:02:42,025
Last, a genetic predisposition has been identified (Locke, 2015; Shungin, 2015).

21
00:02:42,025 --> 00:02:44,612
Figure 51-2.

22
00:02:44,612 --> 00:02:51,787
Prevalence of obesity in the United States by race: United States 2015–2016.

23
00:02:51,787 --> 00:02:55,025
# Adipose Pathophysiology.

24
00:02:55,025 --> 00:02:59,962
Fat tissue is much more complex than merely its energy storage function.

25
00:02:59,962 --> 00:03:12,112
Many fat tissue cells communicate with all other tissues via endocrine and paracrine factors, which are cytokines specifically termed adipokines, lipokines, and exosomal microRNAs .

26
00:03:12,112 --> 00:03:30,737
Some of these with metabolic functions include adiponectin, leptin, tumor necrosis factor α (TNF-α), interleukin 6 (IL-6), resistin, visfatin, apelin, vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF), lipoprotein lipase, and insulin-like growth factor.

27
00:03:30,737 --> 00:03:33,937
Adiponectin is a principal adipokine.

28
00:03:33,937 --> 00:03:42,762
It enhances insulin sensitivity, blocks hepatic glucose release, and has cardioprotective effects on circulating plasma lipids.

29
00:03:42,762 --> 00:03:51,337
An adiponectin deficit is linked with diabetes, hypertension, endothelial cell activation, and cardiovascular disease.

30
00:03:51,337 --> 00:04:01,325
Cytokines that result in insulin resistance are leptin, resistin, TNF-α, and IL-6, and higher levels of these are found during pregnancy.

31
00:04:01,325 --> 00:04:09,237
Indeed, adipokines, especially the inflammatory cytokines, may be the primary stimulant of insulin resistance .

32
00:04:09,237 --> 00:04:17,600
Conversely, adiponectin has antiinflammatory and insulin-sensitizing roles and is negatively regulated by fat mass.

33
00:04:17,600 --> 00:04:24,450
Perivascular adipose tissue also serves as a signaling mediator in regulating vascular function .

34
00:04:24,450 --> 00:04:28,487
Placental production of these adipokines is also important .

35
00:04:28,487 --> 00:04:31,112
# Metabolic Syndrome.

36
00:04:31,112 --> 00:04:39,500
Given its multifaceted endocrine and paracrine functions, the detrimental effects of excessive adipose tissue are not surprising .

37
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:44,675
Obesity interacts with inherited factors to cause insulin resistance.

38
00:04:44,675 --> 00:04:52,075
This resistance is characterized by impaired glucose metabolism and a predisposition to type 2 diabetes.

39
00:04:52,075 --> 00:05:00,925
Insulin resistance also causes several subclinical abnormalities that predispose to cardiovascular disease and accelerate its onset.

40
00:05:00,925 --> 00:05:09,675
The most important among these are type 2 diabetes, dyslipidemia, and hypertension, which are constituents of the metabolic syndrome.

41
00:05:09,675 --> 00:05:14,700
Criteria to define this syndrome are found in Table 51-1.

42
00:05:14,700 --> 00:05:20,750
Waist circumference is the preferred measurement for screening, but any three of five factors listed

43
00:05:20,908 --> 00:05:24,483
are sufficient to diagnose the metabolic syndrome.

44
00:05:24,433 --> 00:05:31,033
Notably, most patients with type 2 diabetes have metabolic syndrome according to these criteria.

45
00:05:31,033 --> 00:05:37,545
Also, obese women with hypertension typically demonstrate elevated plasma insulin levels.

46
00:05:37,545 --> 00:05:40,133
Table 51-1.

47
00:05:40,133 --> 00:05:55,333
Criteria for Diagnosis of the Metabolic Syndrome The National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES) of the CDC documented an overall prevalence of 34 percent for the metabolic syndrome in the United States by 2012 .

48
00:05:55,333 --> 00:05:58,858
As expected, the prevalence rose with age.

49
00:05:58,858 --> 00:06:06,870
It was 20 percent for those aged 18 to 29 years and was 36 percent for those aged 30 to 49 years.

50
00:06:06,870 --> 00:06:10,308
# Nonalcoholic Fatty Liver Disease.

51
00:06:10,308 --> 00:06:15,633
Generally speaking, visceral adiposity correlates with hepatic fat content .

52
00:06:15,633 --> 00:06:21,608
With obesity, excessive fat accumulates in the liver and is termed hepatic steatosis.

53
00:06:21,608 --> 00:06:26,583
This is also called nonalcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD).

54
00:06:26,583 --> 00:06:38,033
In persons with the metabolic syndrome, steatosis can progress to nonalcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH) and then potentially to cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma.

55
00:06:38,033 --> 00:06:47,695
It is the major cause of cirrhosis and liver cancer, and annual medical costs of NAFLD in the United States exceed $100 billion .

56
00:06:47,695 --> 00:06:53,433
Moreover, NAFLD is strongly associated with cardiovascular disease .

57
00:06:53,433 --> 00:06:59,083
These interactions are explored further in Chapter 58 (p. 1040).

58
00:06:59,083 --> 00:07:02,383
# Obesity-associated Morbidity.

59
00:07:02,383 --> 00:07:11,220
Obese individuals suffer well-known consequences such as glucose intolerance, hypertension, dyslipidemia, and metabolic syndrome.

60
00:07:11,220 --> 00:07:22,058
Furthermore, metabolic syndrome and obesity are linked with cardiovascular disease, including myocardial infarction, atrial fibrillation, heart failure, and stroke .

61
00:07:22,058 --> 00:07:29,170
Excessive adiposity raises blood pressure and accounts for up to 75 percent of primary hypertension .

62
00:07:29,170 --> 00:07:38,408
Insulin resistance and metabolic syndrome cause structural cerebral changes in the hippocampus and lower executive functioning and memory in adults .

63
00:07:38,408 --> 00:07:43,295
And cytokine-induced osteoarthritis has long been linked to obesity .

64
00:07:43,295 --> 00:07:50,545
Because of the foregoing, it is not surprising that obesity is associated with higher rates of all-cause early mortality.

65
00:07:50,545 --> 00:07:57,520
Cardiovascular mortality data from 19 prospective studies are shown in Figure 51-3 .

66
00:07:57,520 --> 00:08:05,808
In these and other studies, mortality risk from cardiovascular disease and cancer grew proportionally with increasing BMI.

67
00:08:05,808 --> 00:08:14,358
However, an obesity paradox—whereby certain groups actually derive a survival advantage from being obese—is hypothesized .

68
00:08:14,358 --> 00:08:19,420
Despite this, the health benefits of weight normalization are well documented .

69
00:08:19,420 --> 00:08:22,070
Figure 51-3.

70
00:08:22,070 --> 00:08:33,970
Estimated hazard ratios (95% CI) for death due to cardiovascular disease according to body mass index among 1.46 million white adult men and women.

71
00:08:33,970 --> 00:08:36,533
# Obesity Treatment.

72
00:08:36,533 --> 00:08:39,433
Weight loss is tremendously difficult.

73
00:08:39,433 --> 00:08:44,245
If achieved, long-term maintenance poses equally daunting challenges.

74
00:08:44,245 --> 00:08:49,820
Obstetrician-gynecologists are encouraged to aid weight loss in obese adult women.

75
00:08:49,820 --> 00:09:01,258
Successful approaches include behavioral, pharmacological, and surgical techniques or a combination of these (Dixon, 2016; Heymsfield, 2017).

76
00:09:01,258 --> 00:09:11,145
Dietary changes and exercise reduce weight and rates of the associated metabolic syndrome (Garvey, 2016; Martin, 2018).

77
00:09:11,145 --> 00:09:18,420
When used in conjunction with bariatric surgery, glucose control in those with type 2 diabetes is improved.

78
00:09:18,420 --> 00:09:25,445
However, both surgical and medical interventions are associated with appreciable long-term failure rates.

79
00:09:25,445 --> 00:09:32,208
Indeed, the failure rate is 50 percent in patients with diabetes undergoing bariatric surgery .

80
00:09:32,208 --> 00:09:34,545
Pregnancy And Obesity.

81
00:09:34,545 --> 00:09:38,933
Obese women unequivocally have reproductive disadvantages .

82
00:09:38,933 --> 00:09:53,483
This translates into difficulty in achieving pregnancy, early and recurrent pregnancy loss, preterm delivery, and several obstetrical, medical, and surgical complications with pregnancy, labor, delivery, and the puerperium .

83
00:09:53,483 --> 00:09:59,770
Also, oral contraceptive pill failure and associated thromboembolism may be more likely.

84
00:09:59,770 --> 00:10:06,183
Second-trimester surgical abortion in obese women also carries an increased complication risk .

85
00:10:06,183 --> 00:10:16,845
Last, infants—and later, adult children—of obese mothers have correspondingly higher morbidity rates (Godfrey, 2017; He, 2020).

86
00:10:16,845 --> 00:10:19,158
Obesity complicating

87
00:10:19,324 --> 00:10:22,886
pregnancy has grown substantially in this country.

88
00:10:22,836 --> 00:10:29,311
Our experiences at Parkland Hospital over three epochs are shown in Figure 51-4.

89
00:10:29,311 --> 00:10:36,386
Most recently, obese women constitute >60 percent of pregnant gravidas in our health system.

90
00:10:36,386 --> 00:10:39,011
Figure 51-4.

91
00:10:39,011 --> 00:10:47,674
Increasing prevalence of obesity during four epochs in pregnant women classified at the time of their first prenatal visit at Parkland Hospital.

92
00:10:47,674 --> 00:10:50,286
# Maternal Morbidity.

93
00:10:50,286 --> 00:10:55,574
For overweight women, higher rates of adverse outcomes complicate pregnancy.

94
00:10:55,574 --> 00:11:03,249
Shown in Table 51-2 are results from five studies including more than 1 million singleton pregnancies.

95
00:11:03,249 --> 00:11:13,399
Although not as magnified as in the obese cohort, rates of almost all complications are significantly greater in overweight women than in those with normal BMI.

96
00:11:13,399 --> 00:11:15,974
Table 51-2.

97
00:11:15,974 --> 00:11:38,649
Adverse Pregnancy Effects in Overweight and Obese Women For obese women, definitions used in studies of adverse outcomes vary widely, and BMIs from >30 kg/m2 to >50 kg/m2 have served as thresholds (Crane, 2013; Pratt, 2020; Stamilio, 2014).

98
00:11:38,649 --> 00:11:49,136
In one study, Mariona (2017) reviewed maternal deaths in Michigan and found that the maternal mortality rate was nearly fourfold higher in obese women.

99
00:11:49,136 --> 00:12:10,074
Women with supermorbid obesity experience very high rates of maternal and neonatal complications including preeclampsia, macrosomia, and cesarean delivery, along with higher rates of neonatal meconium aspiration, ventilator support, and neonatal death (Marshall, 2014; Smid, 2016).

100
00:12:10,074 --> 00:12:14,811
Data from one large study are shown in Figure 51-5 .

101
00:12:14,811 --> 00:12:17,549
Figure 51-5.

102
00:12:17,549 --> 00:12:27,436
Incidence of selected pregnancy outcomes in women enrolled in the FASTER (Firstand Second-Trimester Evaluation of Risk) trial according to BMI.

103
00:12:27,436 --> 00:12:33,649
Especially striking are the markedly elevated rates of hypertension and gestational diabetes.

104
00:12:33,649 --> 00:12:46,536
As discussed previously, obesity and the metabolic syndrome are characterized by insulin resistance, which creates low-grade inflammation, endothelial activation, and increased sodium reabsorption .

105
00:12:46,536 --> 00:12:50,799
These latter effects play a central role in preeclampsia .

106
00:12:50,799 --> 00:12:59,824
The overwhelming association between rising maternal BMI and the incidence of preeclampsia is depicted in Figure 51-6 .

107
00:12:59,824 --> 00:13:06,661
The risks for hypertension are highest in women with a BMI >50 kg/m2 .

108
00:13:06,661 --> 00:13:16,849
Similar observations were reported from a large Canadian study and by the Safe Labor Consortium (Kim, 2016; Schummers, 2015).

109
00:13:16,849 --> 00:13:19,574
Figure 51-6.

110
00:13:19,574 --> 00:13:27,636
Hyperglycemia and Adverse Pregnancy Outcome (HAPO) study: frequency of preeclampsia according to BMI.

111
00:13:27,636 --> 00:13:32,899
Obesity and hypertension are common cofactors in peripartum heart failure .

112
00:13:32,899 --> 00:13:42,886
Stewart and colleagues (2016) used magnetic resonance imaging to prospectively study the effect of obesity on cardiac remodeling in pregnancy.

113
00:13:42,886 --> 00:13:49,261
Concentric remodeling, which was considered abnormal, was greater in overweight or obese women .

114
00:13:49,261 --> 00:13:53,761
This, however, regressed to normal by 3 months postpartum.

115
00:13:53,761 --> 00:14:04,499
Triebwasser and coworkers (2019) found that women with abnormal cardiac remodeling had a higher incidence of pregnancy-associated hypertensive disorders.

116
00:14:04,499 --> 00:14:07,186
Figure 51-7.

117
00:14:07,186 --> 00:14:15,611
Geometric changes of ventricular remodeling across pregnancy in obese and normalweight women determined by cardiac MR imaging.

118
00:14:15,611 --> 00:14:22,374
LVM = left ventricular mass, LVEDV = left ventricular end-diastolic volume.

119
00:14:22,374 --> 00:14:28,874
Obesity and gestational diabetes are inextricably linked as shown in Table 51-2.

120
00:14:28,874 --> 00:14:36,599
Their coexistence and associated adverse pregnancy outcomes are discussed in Chapter 60 (p. 1068).

121
00:14:36,599 --> 00:14:42,511
Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease is associated with several adverse pregnancy outcomes .

122
00:14:42,511 --> 00:14:56,086
In a cohort of women with NAFLD, risks for preeclampsia, preterm birth, lowbirthweight neonates, cesarean delivery, and gestational diabetes were elevated (Hagström, 2016).

123
00:14:56,086 --> 00:15:09,911
In one prospective study of 476 pregnancies, first-trimester sonographic evidence of maternal NAFLD was strongly associated with gestational diabetes (De Souza, 2016a,b).

124
00:15:09,911 --> 00:15:22,224
Meyer and associates (2013) found that overweight and obese gravidas had a higher proportion of low-density lipoprotein III (LDL-III) compared with that of normal-weight women.

125
00:15:22,224 --> 00:15:29,361
LDL-III predominance is a hallmark of the ectopic liver fat accumulation that is typical of NAFLD.

126
00:15:29,361 --> 00:15:31,261
At Parkland

127
00:15:31,420 --> 00:15:43,007
Hospital, we are now frequently encountering obese gravidas who have NAFLD and evidence of steatohepatitis manifest by elevated serum hepatic transaminase levels.

128
00:15:42,957 --> 00:15:49,245
In rare cases, liver biopsy is necessary to exclude other causes for these findings.

129
00:15:49,245 --> 00:15:54,707
Quality-of-life measures also are negatively affected by obesity during pregnancy .

130
00:15:54,707 --> 00:16:02,782
One systematic review found significantly higher risks of depression in overweight and obese women during and after pregnancy .

131
00:16:02,782 --> 00:16:08,820
Obese women were also significantly more likely to experience anxiety during pregnancy.

132
00:16:08,820 --> 00:16:11,582
# Perinatal Mortality.

133
00:16:11,582 --> 00:16:15,920
Stillbirths are more prevalent as the degree of obesity accrues .

134
00:16:15,920 --> 00:16:23,232
In a review of almost 100 studies, obesity was the highest-ranking modifiable risk factor for stillbirth .

135
00:16:23,232 --> 00:16:38,570
In women with supermorbid obesity compared with normal-weight gravidas, Yao and associates (2014) found 5.7 and 13.6-fold higher stillbirth rates at 39 and 41 weeks’ gestation, respectively.

136
00:16:38,570 --> 00:16:44,457
Remarkably, 25 percent of term stillbirths in this study involved obese women.

137
00:16:44,457 --> 00:16:51,957
Chronic hypertension with superimposed preeclampsia associated with obesity is one cause of excessive stillbirths.

138
00:16:51,957 --> 00:16:58,920
These fetal deaths may be associated with placental lesions of decidual arteriopathy and infarctions .

139
00:16:58,920 --> 00:17:09,107
Evaluating perinatal death rates, Lindam and coworkers (2016) reported that high maternal BMI in early pregnancy was a risk factor.

140
00:17:09,107 --> 00:17:17,595
The risk of neonatal death also is greater for obese women (Johansson, 2014; Meehan, 2014).

141
00:17:17,595 --> 00:17:28,607
Last, one study found that accruing weight between pregnancies is a risk factor for perinatal mortality, whereas weight loss between pregnancies for overweight women lowers this risk .

142
00:17:28,607 --> 00:17:31,345
# Perinatal Morbidity.

143
00:17:31,345 --> 00:17:36,232
Both fetal and neonatal complication rates are increased in obese women.

144
00:17:36,232 --> 00:17:47,882
Two important and interrelated cofactors that contribute to excessive rates of perinatal morbidity are chronic hypertension and diabetes, both of which are associated with maternal obesity.

145
00:17:47,882 --> 00:17:59,770
These comorbidities each may play a role in the higher rates of fetal-growth restriction and indicated preterm birth that are seen in obese women (Liu, 2019; Tanner, 2020).

146
00:17:59,770 --> 00:18:09,257
Perinates of obese women with preterm prematurely ruptured membranes have a higher incidence of respiratory complications than those born to normal-weight women .

147
00:18:09,257 --> 00:18:20,595
Pregestational diabetes also raises the birth defect rate, and gestational diabetes is complicated by excessive numbers of largefor-gestational-age and macrosomic fetuses .

148
00:18:20,595 --> 00:18:34,832
Even when diabetes and hypertension are not considered, the prevalence of neonatal morbidity is greater in offspring of obese women (Kim, 2016; Polnaszek, 2018; Schummers, 2015).

149
00:18:34,832 --> 00:18:42,345
The propensity for preterm birth may be related to increased adipokines and cytokines causing chronic inflammation .

150
00:18:42,345 --> 00:19:02,720
The group from MetroHealth Medical Center in Cleveland has extensively studied prepregnancy obesity, gestational weight gain, and diabetes and their relationship to adverse pregnancy outcomes and to greater newborn weight and fat mass (Catalano, 2015; Ma, 2016; Yang, 2016).

151
00:19:02,720 --> 00:19:16,970
Although each of these variables is associated with larger and more corpulent newborns, prepregnancy BMI and its effect on inflammation and placental gene expression has the strongest influence on the prevalence of macrosomic neonates.

152
00:19:16,970 --> 00:19:20,845
Rates of birth defects also are higher with obesity .

153
00:19:20,845 --> 00:19:33,420
For neural-tube defects, elevated risks of 1.2-, 1.7-, and 3.1-fold have been described for overweight, obese, and severely obese women, respectively .

154
00:19:33,420 --> 00:19:40,657
The National Birth Defect Prevention Study reported a correlation between BMI and congenital heart defects .

155
00:19:40,657 --> 00:19:45,032
However, this may be related to diabetes as a cofactor .

156
00:19:45,032 --> 00:19:59,795
Importantly, obesity degrades the accuracy of obstetrical ultrasound examination and antepartum identification of birth defects (Adekola, 2015; Dashe, 2009; Yaqub, 2021).

157
00:19:59,795 --> 00:20:03,020
# Long-term Offspring Morbidity.

158
00:20:03,020 --> 00:20:07,995
Obese women beget obese children, who themselves become obese adults.

159
00:20:07,995 --> 00:20:19,382
Catalano and coworkers (2009) studied offspring at a mean age of 9 years and found a direct association with maternal prepregnancy obesity and childhood obesity.

160
00:20:19,382 --> 00:20:22,457
They also reported associations with

161
00:20:22,612 --> 00:20:32,462
central obesity, elevated systolic blood pressure, increased insulin resistance, and lipid abnormalities—all elements of the metabolic syndrome.

162
00:20:32,412 --> 00:20:45,224
Reynolds and associates (2013) reported higher rates of cardiovascular disease and allcause mortality in 37,709 adult offspring of overweight and obese mothers.

163
00:20:45,224 --> 00:20:52,224
Similar cardiometabolic health effects in offspring were echoed by Gaillard and colleagues (2016).

164
00:20:52,224 --> 00:20:58,899
Other data suggest that excessive maternal weight gain in pregnancy may predict obesity in adult offspring .

165
00:20:58,899 --> 00:21:05,637
Last, rates of glucose intolerance and metabolic syndrome are higher among offspring of obese women .

166
00:21:05,637 --> 00:21:10,549
The potential biological mechanisms of these associations are unclear.

167
00:21:10,549 --> 00:21:18,899
But such studies raise the possibility of fetal programming, that is, the fetal environment may lead to adverse adult health outcomes.

168
00:21:18,899 --> 00:21:29,537
Elucidation is limited by insufficient data regarding the influence of maternal and genetic predisposition compared with the diet and activity environment of the infant and child .

169
00:21:29,537 --> 00:21:39,562
The science of epigenetics has provided some support for the possibility that perturbations of the maternal–fetal environment can adversely alter postdelivery events .

170
00:21:39,562 --> 00:21:41,887
Antepartum Management.

171
00:21:41,887 --> 00:21:44,499
# Maternal Weight Gain.

172
00:21:44,499 --> 00:21:54,324
The Institute of Medicine (2009) has updated its previous maternal weight gain determinants (Table 10-4, p. 183).

173
00:21:54,324 --> 00:21:59,612
For overweight women, weight gain of 15 to 25 pounds is suggested.

174
00:21:59,612 --> 00:22:04,912
For obese women, the Institute recommends a gain of 11 to 20 pounds.

175
00:22:04,912 --> 00:22:15,049
Intuitively, maternal weight must increase sufficiently to provide for fetal and placental tissue accrual and for amnionic fluid and maternal blood volume expansion.

176
00:22:15,049 --> 00:22:19,262
Thus, maternal weight loss during pregnancy is discouraged.

177
00:22:19,262 --> 00:22:26,399
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2021) endorses these guidelines.

178
00:22:26,399 --> 00:22:38,749
It is emphasized that these recommendations were issued without firm scientific evidence to support them, and their value remains unproven (Comstock, 2019; Most, 2019).

179
00:22:38,749 --> 00:22:45,424
For example, recent studies differ with respect to the effect of insufficient weight gain for obese women.

180
00:22:45,424 --> 00:22:59,249
Bodnar and colleagues (2016) reported no greater risk for low-birthweight or small-forgestational-age newborns among 47,494 obese women who had inadequate weight gain during pregnancy.

181
00:22:59,249 --> 00:23:07,649
Bogaerts and associates (2015) also found that even weight loss among obese women did not yield poor fetal growth.

182
00:23:07,649 --> 00:23:21,449
In contrast, however, Hannaford and coworkers (2017) reported that obese women who gained less than the Institute recommendations were almost three times more likely to deliver a small-for-gestational-age neonate.

183
00:23:21,449 --> 00:23:30,062
Another study similarly found an almost twofold greater risk of growth-restricted newborns among obese women who lost weight during pregnancy .

184
00:23:30,062 --> 00:23:35,312
Excessive gestational weight gain may portend greater risks for the obese mother.

185
00:23:35,312 --> 00:23:43,987
Berggren and coworkers (2016) noted that overweight and obese women accrued maternal fat with excessive gestational weight gain.

186
00:23:43,987 --> 00:23:58,099
From another analysis, overall higher rates of hypertensive disorders, cesarean delivery, and fetal overgrowth and lower rates of spontaneous preterm birth and fetal undergrowth were found among women gaining more than recommended .

187
00:23:58,099 --> 00:24:15,524
However, when analyzed according to BMI category, significantly higher rates of preeclampsia, cesarean delivery, and fetal overgrowth were identified among the 1937 overweight women, but not the 1445 obese women, who gained excess weight.

188
00:24:15,524 --> 00:24:20,724
Last, overweight and obese women have excessive postpartum weight retention .

189
00:24:20,724 --> 00:24:23,637
# Dietary Intervention.

190
00:24:23,637 --> 00:24:30,337
Several dietary interventions can help limit and achieve the weight gain targets listed in the previous section.

191
00:24:30,337 --> 00:24:35,274
Options include lifestyle changes such as changes in physical activity.

192
00:24:35,274 --> 00:24:42,924
In one randomized trial of exercise in 300 overweight women, risks for gestational diabetes were lowered .

193
00:24:42,924 --> 00:24:53,374
In other trials, however, dietary intervention had no effect on weight gain (Okasene-Gafa, 2019; Seneviratne, 2016).

194
00:24:53,374 --> 00:25:06,162
Also, a Cochrane database analysis of 11,444 women suggests that lifestyle interventions confer only a modest reduction in maternal weight gain, and their benefits for fetal

195
00:25:06,316 --> 00:25:12,603
overgrowth, cesarean delivery rate, and adverse neonatal outcome are not significant .

196
00:25:12,553 --> 00:25:18,653
Last, metformin treatment for obese pregnant women does not improve pregnancy outcomes .

197
00:25:18,653 --> 00:25:27,316
Regarding neonatal outcomes, the poor success of lifestyle interventions during pregnancy has been attributed to their late introduction.

198
00:25:27,316 --> 00:25:33,916
In this regard, it is presumed that early gene expression within the placenta has already been programmed .

199
00:25:33,916 --> 00:25:36,353
# Prenatal Care.

200
00:25:36,353 --> 00:25:42,078
Close prenatal monitoring detects most early signs of diabetes or hypertension.

201
00:25:42,078 --> 00:25:48,466
Early gestational diabetes screening did not result in less morbidity compared with standard screening .

202
00:25:48,466 --> 00:25:52,178
Obstructive sleep apnea is common in obese women .

203
00:25:52,178 --> 00:26:01,328
Standard screening tests for fetal anomalies are sufficient, while remembering the sonographic limitations for fetal anomaly detection in this group.

204
00:26:01,328 --> 00:26:07,766
Even so, ultrasound predicts fetal weight accurately in these women (O’Brien, 2020).

205
00:26:07,766 --> 00:26:14,116
Accurate fetal-growth surveillance in obese women usually requires serial sonographic assessment.

206
00:26:14,116 --> 00:26:26,616
Dude and coworkers (2019) reported that ultrasound as a detection tool had high specificity but poor sensitivity for growth restriction and a low positive predictive value for macrosomia.

207
00:26:26,616 --> 00:26:31,741
Antepartum external fetal heart rate monitoring is likewise more difficult.

208
00:26:31,741 --> 00:26:34,003
Intrapartum Management.

209
00:26:34,003 --> 00:26:39,716
Obesity poses increased risk for multiple labor or intrapartum complications.

210
00:26:39,716 --> 00:26:48,103
These include postterm pregnancy or labor abnormalities (Carpenter, 2016; Shenouda, 2020).

211
00:26:48,103 --> 00:26:58,778
In one study of 143,519 women, the odds of spontaneous labor at term in obese women was approximately half that of normal-weight women .

212
00:26:58,778 --> 00:27:10,466
In an analysis of more than 5000 parturients, women with a BMI >30 kg/m2 had a longer duration and slower early progression of first-stage labor .

213
00:27:10,466 --> 00:27:18,403
Epidural analgesia apparently does not affect the length of labor in obese women (Polónia Valente, 2020).

214
00:27:18,403 --> 00:27:20,903
# Labor Induction.

215
00:27:20,903 --> 00:27:29,591
As shown in Table 51-2, compared with normal-weight women, obese women are nearly twice as likely to undergo labor induction .

216
00:27:29,591 --> 00:27:37,378
However, obese women are more likely to experience a failed induction, and this risk rises with increasing obesity .

217
00:27:37,378 --> 00:27:45,341
The duration of labor resulting in vaginal delivery lengthens with increasing maternal BMI (Carlhäll, 2020).

218
00:27:45,341 --> 00:28:03,666
In a retrospective analysis of 470 nulliparous women with a BMI >30 kg/m2, those who underwent labor induction at 39 weeks’ gestation had a greater cesarean delivery rate—26 versus 40 percent compared with a cohort expectantly managed .

219
00:28:03,666 --> 00:28:12,966
In a study of 485 women with class III obesity, the overall cesarean delivery rate after failed induction was 49 percent .

220
00:28:12,966 --> 00:28:24,278
The rate was 46 percent for those with a BMI 30–40, 63 percent for a BMI 40–50, and 69 percent for a BMI >60.

221
00:28:24,278 --> 00:28:28,903
Although perhaps not unexpected, these findings have been challenged.

222
00:28:28,903 --> 00:28:45,203
Lee and associates (2016) reviewed statistics from 74,725 deliveries in obese women and reported that elective induction at 37 to 39 weeks’ gestation was actually associated with a lower cesarean delivery rate.

223
00:28:45,203 --> 00:29:02,678
Additionally, in a secondary analysis of one randomized trial, obese nulliparas who were randomly assigned to Foley catheter cervical ripening plus misoprostol for labor induction had a reduced labor duration and lower rate of cesarean delivery compared with those given solely misoprostol .

224
00:29:02,678 --> 00:29:12,078
These conflicting results highlight the difficulties faced by obstetricians as they contemplate the seemingly competing interests of the fetus and the obese mother.

225
00:29:12,078 --> 00:29:14,778
# Anesthesia Risks.

226
00:29:14,778 --> 00:29:24,953
Obese women present anesthesia challenges that include difficult epidural and spinal analgesia placement and complications from failed or difficult intubations.

227
00:29:24,953 --> 00:29:34,328
Evaluation of gravidas with supermorbid obesity by the anesthesiologist is recommended during prenatal care or upon arrival to the labor unit .

228
00:29:34,328 --> 00:29:42,953
Regional analgesia for morbidly obese women is associated with longer neuraxial procedure times and more failed placement attempts .

229
00:29:42,953 --> 00:29:52,616
Importantly, however, spinal analgesia in obese women for cesarean delivery does not appear to have greater benefits than combined spinal-epidural.

230
00:29:52,616 --> 00:29:54,828
One study comparing

231
00:29:54,988 --> 00:30:05,988
single-shot spinal analgesia and combined spinal-epidural analgesia found that both methods could be placed with equal expediency and efficacy in morbidly obese gravidas .

232
00:30:05,938 --> 00:30:18,750
Obese women who undergo neuraxial analgesia that is complicated by relative hypotension more frequently have neonates with umbilical artery cord blood acidemia, which probably stems from delayed delivery.

233
00:30:18,750 --> 00:30:27,863
One study of 572 obese women showed that cord blood pH significantly dropped and base deficit rose with increasing BMI .

234
00:30:27,863 --> 00:30:47,013
The rate of gases with a pH <7.1 doubled from 3.5 percent for a BMI <25 kg/m2 to 7.1 percent for a BMI ≥40 kg/m2. Anesthetic risks and complications are discussed in Chapter 25.

235
00:30:47,013 --> 00:30:49,700
# Cesarean Delivery.

236
00:30:49,700 --> 00:30:58,775
As shown in Table 51-2, rates of cesarean delivery are significantly greater in obese women compared with normal-weight gravidas.

237
00:30:58,775 --> 00:31:15,975
In an analysis of 226,958 women, cesarean delivery rates rose significantly for overweight (34 percent), class I (38 percent), class II (43 percent), and class III (50 percent) obesity .

238
00:31:15,975 --> 00:31:19,725
Our experiences at Parkland Hospital are similar .

239
00:31:19,725 --> 00:31:31,113
Moreover, obese women had less composite morbidity when labor was attempted compared with cesarean delivery (Gibbs Pickens, 2018; Grasch, 2017).

240
00:31:31,113 --> 00:31:45,225
More worrisome is that obese women also have higher rates of emergency cesarean delivery, and obesity lengthens times for decision-to-incision and for delivery (Girsen, 2014; Pulman, 2015).

241
00:31:45,225 --> 00:31:47,800
Figure 51-8.

242
00:31:47,800 --> 00:31:56,600
Cesarean delivery rates in more than 300,000 women according to BMI categories over a 30-year period at Parkland Hospital.

243
00:31:56,600 --> 00:32:09,438
Discussed in Chapter 31 (p. 577), the incidence of failed trial of labor after cesarean is higher in obese women (Grasch, 2017; Wilson, 2020).

244
00:32:09,438 --> 00:32:16,438
Women who gain weight between pregnancies also have significantly lower rates of vaginal birth after cesarean.

245
00:32:16,438 --> 00:32:19,200
# Surgical Concerns.

246
00:32:19,200 --> 00:32:29,250
For cesarean delivery, forethought is given to optimal placement and type of abdominal incision to provide access to the fetus and to effect the best wound closure.

247
00:32:29,250 --> 00:32:34,700
We prefer a vertical incision in obese women to provide the most direct access .

248
00:32:34,700 --> 00:32:45,325
Others prefer a low transverse abdominal incision, with or without rostral taping of the pendulous abdomen (Karimyar, 2020; Lakhi, 2018).

249
00:32:45,325 --> 00:32:51,500
Individual differences in maternal body habitus preclude naming any one approach as superior .

250
00:32:51,500 --> 00:33:07,250
Some observational studies have compared wound outcomes associated with vertical and transverse skin incisions, but results are conflicting as to a superior option (Karimyar, 2020; Marrs, 2014; Sutton, 2016).

251
00:33:07,250 --> 00:33:09,875
Figure 51-9.

252
00:33:09,875 --> 00:33:13,025
Abdominal incision for the obese woman.

253
00:33:13,025 --> 00:33:14,925
A. Frontal view.

254
00:33:14,925 --> 00:33:21,425
The dotted line indicates an appropriate skin incision for abdominal entry relative to the panniculus.

255
00:33:21,425 --> 00:33:29,138
As shown by the uterus in the background, selection of this periumbilical site permits access to the lower uterine segment.

256
00:33:29,138 --> 00:33:31,438
B. Sagittal view.

257
00:33:31,438 --> 00:33:36,775
The frequency of abdominal wound infections is directly related to BMI.

258
00:33:36,775 --> 00:33:49,525
Conner and associates (2014) found the risk of wound infection is threefold higher—23 versus 7 percent—in women with supermorbid obesity compared with nonobese patients.

259
00:33:49,525 --> 00:34:03,063
Among women with a BMI >45 kg/m2, reported wound complication rates range from 14 to 19 percent (Smid, 2015; Stamilio, 2014).

260
00:34:03,063 --> 00:34:06,850
Comorbid diabetes apparently raises this risk .

261
00:34:06,850 --> 00:34:21,275
Other studies describe wound complication rates ranging from 2 to >40 percent in obese women (Conner, 2014; Marrs, 2014; Smid, 2015; Thornburg, 2012).

262
00:34:21,275 --> 00:34:24,450
Several interventions may be preventive.

263
00:34:24,450 --> 00:34:30,950
Closure of subcutaneous tissue when at least 2 cm deep reduces wound complication rates .

264
00:34:30,950 --> 00:34:36,138
Steel staples and subcuticular skin closure produce identical results .

265
00:34:36,138 --> 00:34:42,588
Studies have also examined the use of higher doses of perioperative prophylactic antibiotics.

266
00:34:42,588 --> 00:34:54,538
Pharmacokinetic studies indicate that tissue concentrations of prophylactic antibiotics are lower with increasing BMI (Pevzner, 2011; Young, 2015).

267
00:34:54,538 --> 00:35:02,863
One prospective study showed that a 3-g dose of cefazolin resulted in higher tissue concentrations compared with a 2-g dose .

268
00:35:02,863 --> 00:35:11,863
A retrospective analysis of 335 women with a median weight of 310 pounds found that the higher dose of cefazolin did not

269
00:35:12,028 --> 00:35:16,365
result in fewer surgical site infections (SSIs) .

270
00:35:16,315 --> 00:35:29,028
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020) recognizes either 2- or 3g doses of cefazolin as suitable for those with weights ≥80 kg.

271
00:35:29,028 --> 00:35:43,503
The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention recommends a 2-g dose for weights ≥80 kg and 3-g dose for those ≥120 kg (Berríos-Torres, 2017).

272
00:35:43,503 --> 00:35:52,953
One pharmacokinetic analysis in obese women showed sufficient tissue levels with a 2-g dose for cesarean deliveries lasting 1.5 hours.

273
00:35:52,953 --> 00:35:58,715
Authors recommended consideration for redosing in obese women if surgeries were longer .

274
00:35:58,715 --> 00:36:08,865
Last, some early evidence may support extending oral antibiotic prophylaxis for 48 hours postcesarean in obese women to lower SSIs .

275
00:36:08,865 --> 00:36:26,890
Obese women administered preoperative cephalosporin prophylaxis had a surgical infection rate of 13.4 percent compared with a rate of 6.4 percent for those given a 2-day postoperative course of oral cephalexin and metronidazole in addition to routine preoperative prophylaxis.

276
00:36:26,890 --> 00:36:33,040
Negative-pressure wound therapy (NPWT) also has been used prophylactically .

277
00:36:33,040 --> 00:36:45,528
To address this, Hussamy and colleagues (2019) designed a randomized trial of NPWT versus routine dressing in 441 obese women undergoing cesarean delivery.

278
00:36:45,528 --> 00:36:55,490
Such therapy did not significantly lower the postoperative wound complication rate compared with routine care—17 versus 19 percent, respectively.

279
00:36:55,490 --> 00:36:59,003
A subsequent report confirmed these findings .

280
00:36:59,003 --> 00:37:06,228
For the obese woman who is delivered vaginally, puerperal tubal sterilization is safe regardless of BMI .

281
00:37:06,228 --> 00:37:09,965
These procedures are described in Chapter 39.

282
00:37:09,965 --> 00:37:15,153
The risks for postpartum venous thromboembolism are increased in obese women.

283
00:37:15,153 --> 00:37:21,740
This is despite the fact that velocity of lowerextremity blood flow is increased in obese pregnant women .

284
00:37:21,740 --> 00:37:28,503
As discussed in Chapter 55 (p. 990), thromboprophylaxis is controversial.

285
00:37:28,503 --> 00:37:44,378
To lower thromboembolic complications, graduated compression stockings, hydration, and early mobilization after cesarean delivery in obese women are recommended by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019b).

286
00:37:44,378 --> 00:37:53,790
If there are additional risk factors other than class III obesity, the Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2020) recommends enoxaparin.

287
00:37:53,790 --> 00:37:59,590
We do not routinely use thromboprophylaxis for obesity alone at Parkland Hospital.

288
00:37:59,590 --> 00:38:05,115
This practice has recently been confirmed by Lu and associates (2021).

289
00:38:05,115 --> 00:38:07,365
Bariatric Surgery.

290
00:38:07,365 --> 00:38:19,340
Several surgical procedures are designed to treat morbid obesity either by diminishing gastric volume (restrictive) or by bypassing gastrointestinal absorption (restrictive malabsorptive).

291
00:38:19,340 --> 00:38:32,278
In nonpregnant patients, these procedures serve to improve or resolve diabetes, hyperlipidemia, hypertension, and obstructive sleep apnea and to reduce risks of myocardial infarction and death .

292
00:38:32,278 --> 00:38:35,153
# Restrictive Procedures.

293
00:38:35,153 --> 00:38:49,865
Of options, the approved laparoscopic adjustable silicone gastric banding (LASGB) procedure—LAPBAND—places a band 2 cm below the gastroesophageal junction to create a small stomach pouch above the ring.

294
00:38:49,865 --> 00:38:54,253
The ring diameter is controlled by a saline reservoir in the band.

295
00:38:54,253 --> 00:38:58,878
These procedures can have positive effects on pregnancy outcomes.

296
00:38:58,878 --> 00:39:06,440
One study compared pregnancy outcomes in bariatric surgery patients whose surgery was done after the first pregnancy.

297
00:39:06,440 --> 00:39:17,203
Following bariatric surgery, the incidences of hypertension, gestational diabetes, and preterm birth were significantly lower in the bariatric surgery patients .

298
00:39:17,203 --> 00:39:22,340
The results from these and other studies are shown in Table 51-3.

299
00:39:22,340 --> 00:39:24,940
Table 51-3.

300
00:39:24,940 --> 00:39:32,715
Pregnancy Outcomes Following Bariatric Surgery Deflation of the band during pregnancy affects maternal and fetal weight gain.

301
00:39:32,715 --> 00:39:47,840
Pilone and coworkers (2014) studied 22 pregnancies after band placement and reported that all women underwent full deflation of the band in the first trimester and gained an average of 14.7 kg during pregnancy.

302
00:39:47,840 --> 00:39:55,503
In another study, 42 women underwent deflation of the band, whereas 54 women maintained band inflation.

303
00:39:55,503 --> 00:40:12,790
A deflated band was associated with higher mean maternal weight gain—15.4 kg versus 7.6 kg, increased birthweight—3712 versus 3380 g, and a twofold greater risk of macrosomia

304
00:40:12,940 --> 00:40:15,565
compared with an inflated band .

305
00:40:15,515 --> 00:40:28,640
Rarely, the band may slip due to nausea and vomiting, especially with advancing gestation or after delivery (Pilone, 2014; Schmitt, 2016; Suffee, 2012).

306
00:40:28,640 --> 00:40:39,627
One fetus suffered a fatal cerebral hemorrhage caused by maternal vitamin K deficiency secondary to prolonged vomiting due to band slippage that created a gastric outlet obstruction .

307
00:40:39,627 --> 00:40:43,290
# Restrictive Malabsorptive Procedures.

308
00:40:43,290 --> 00:40:52,265
The laparoscopically performed Roux-en-Y gastric bypass is the most commonly used procedure for gastric restriction and selective malabsorption.

309
00:40:52,265 --> 00:40:57,327
Pregnancy outcomes are changed remarkably following Roux-en-Y bypass .

310
00:40:57,327 --> 00:41:05,677
As shown in Table 51-3, rates of hypertension, gestational diabetes, and fetal macrosomia are reduced.

311
00:41:05,677 --> 00:41:20,252
Serious complications with bypass operations are uncommon, however, upper abdominal pain is frequent in pregnancy and may be associated with internal herniation, which is protrusion of the bowel through a window defect in the mesentery.

312
00:41:20,252 --> 00:41:28,752
Petersen and associates (2017) described outcomes in a birth cohort including 139 pregnancies.

313
00:41:28,752 --> 00:41:35,240
Upper abdominal pain complicated 46 percent, and a third of these had internal herniation.

314
00:41:35,240 --> 00:41:43,690
The preterm birth rate was 14 of 64 among those with upper abdominal pain versus 1 of 75 in those without pain.

315
00:41:43,690 --> 00:41:56,115
Intussusception and small bowel obstruction can develop from internal herniation, and maternal deaths from herniation and obstruction are reported (Moore, 2004; Renault, 2012).

316
00:41:56,115 --> 00:42:05,040
Bowel obstruction is notoriously difficult to diagnose during pregnancy (Vannevel, 2016; Wax, 2013).

317
00:42:05,040 --> 00:42:07,290
# Pregnancy.

318
00:42:07,290 --> 00:42:15,340
Because of its associated health successes, bariatric surgery is popular, and many women subsequently become pregnant .

319
00:42:15,340 --> 00:42:30,827
From studies, fertility improves and obstetrical complication rates decline in women after bariatric surgery compared with morbidly obese controls (Getahun, 2021; Kominiarek, 2017; Yi, 2015).

320
00:42:30,827 --> 00:42:40,590
In one of these studies, despite surgical treatment, almost half of 670 women were still obese at the time of their first pregnancy after bypass .

321
00:42:40,590 --> 00:42:53,427
Nevertheless, the frequency of large-for-gestational-age newborns dropped from 22 to 8.6 percent and of small-for-gestational-age neonates rose from 7.6 to 15.6 percent.

322
00:42:53,427 --> 00:43:01,577
In a systematic review, Kwong and colleagues (2018) confirmed these fetal weight trends after bariatric surgery.

323
00:43:01,577 --> 00:43:11,902
According to Maric and colleagues (2020), fetuses are smaller because of lower maternal glucose levels rather than alterations of the fetoplacental circulation.

324
00:43:11,902 --> 00:43:16,477
Also, risks for diabetes and preeclampsia were reduced.

325
00:43:16,477 --> 00:43:25,840
Last, evidence suggests that the risk for congenital anomalies is decreased (Auger, 2019; Neovius, 2019).

326
00:43:25,840 --> 00:43:38,502
Currently, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019b) recommends that women who have undergone bariatric surgery be assessed for vitamin and nutritional sufficiency.

327
00:43:38,502 --> 00:43:45,277
When indicated, vitamins B12 and D, folic acid, and calcium supplementation are given.

328
00:43:45,277 --> 00:43:48,590
Vitamin A deficiency also is possible .

329
00:43:48,590 --> 00:43:56,427
Women with a gastric band should be monitored by their bariatric team during pregnancy because band adjustments may be necessary.

330
00:43:56,427 --> 00:44:07,065
Last, special vigilance for signs of internal herniation with intestinal obstruction is encouraged (Stuart, 2017; Wax, 2013).

